<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Firishta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1560–c. 1620</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Ferishta, Muhammad Qasim Hindu Shah, Muhammad Qasim Ferishta, Muhammad Qasim Firishta</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Firishta, Muhammad Qasim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> Shah, was a Persian historian born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgan">Astarabad</a> who served the Nizam Shahi court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadnagar">Ahmadnagar</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-adil-shah-ii">Ibrahim Adil Shah II</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>. His Tarikh-i Firishta, completed in the early seventeenth century, is a history of Muslim rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the main narrative source for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-sultanates">Deccan sultanates</a>, though it relies partly on legend.</p></div>
